To send custom user inputs, payloads, and headers to a server, you need to look at the entire landscape of data transmission methods available in computing.
Beyond standard penetration testing tools, data can be transmitted to a web server via Interception Proxies, Command-Line Utilities, Browser Extensions, Network Utilities, Scripting Libraries, API Clients, and Automated Frameworks.
Here is every single category and tool that exists to send data to a server:
------------------------------
## 1. Interception Proxies & Replayers (Best for Payload Modification)
These tools capture live web traffic out of your browser or application, allowing you to manually modify parameters, inject bypass strings, and replay the request. [1] 

* Burp Suite (Repeater & Intruder): The definitive web penetration testing suite.
* Caido: A lightweight, high-performance proxy written in Rust.
* OWASP ZAP: A completely free, open-source proxy alternative.
* Charles Proxy / Fiddler: General-purpose debugging proxies used widely for mobile app and web data tracking.

## 2. Command-Line HTTP Clients (Best for Quick Testing)
These are terminal-based utilities engineered specifically to format and transmit raw strings, headers, and files directly to a remote host.

* curl: The industry standard for transferring data with URLs. It supports almost every protocol and proxy option.
* HTTPie: A modern command-line HTTP client with intuitive syntax and built-in JSON formatting.
* wget: A CLI utility that can send data via POST arguments (--post-data).
* Fetch (Node CLI) / Deno: Server-side JavaScript environments that execute fast, scriptable data transmissions natively. [2, 3, 4, 5, 6] 

## 3. Web Browser Extensions (Best for Manual Browsing)
If you want to inject custom headers (like your HackerOne identifier) or bypass payloads globally while casually navigating a website through Google Chrome or Mozilla Firefox. [7] 

* ModHeader / Modify Header Value: Lets you add, modify, or filter HTTP request headers on the fly.
* HackBar: A browser-embedded panel designed for pen testers to quickly insert SQLi, XSS, and encoding payloads into URL parameters.
* Cookie-Editor: Allows you to manually alter, inject, or delete session data sent to the server. [8] 

## 4. Raw Network Utilities (Best for Non-HTTP or Custom Packets)
When you need to drop down to the TCP/UDP layer to send raw, unformatted text strings or custom binary data that standard web clients would reject.

* Netcat (nc): Known as the network Swiss Army knife. You can type raw HTTP headers line-by-line and stream them straight to a port.
* Socat: A more advanced version of Netcat that establishes two independent bidirectional data streams.
* Ncat: Nmap's modern implementation of Netcat, featuring SSL/TLS support for sending payloads to HTTPS servers.
* Scapy: A powerful interactive packet manipulation library written in Python to forge custom network packets. [9, 10, 11, 12, 13] 

## 5. API Development Clients (Best for Structured Data)
Built primarily for developers but highly utilized by security researchers to build complex, authenticated JSON, XML, GraphQL, or gRPC data payloads.

* Postman: A highly interactive GUI platform for constructing and organizing API requests.
* Hoppscotch: A free, web-based, fast API repository client.
* Insomnia: A sleek desktop application for debugging and sending REST, GraphQL, and gRPC inputs.
* Thunder Client: A lightweight REST API client extension built directly inside VS Code. [14, 15, 16, 17] 

## 6. Automated Directory, Parameter, & Vulnerability Fuzzers
These tools ingest massive text files (wordlists) and programmatically inject those strings into server endpoints at high speeds.

* ffuf (Fuzz Faster U Fool): High-speed CLI web fuzzer utilizing specific placeholder injection.
* Nuclei: A fast, template-driven network and web vulnerability scanner.
* Gobuster / Dirsearch: High-efficiency directory and file enumerators.
* Arjun: Finds hidden parameters on a server by guessing and injecting inputs heuristically. [18, 19, 20] 

## 7. Programming Language Libraries (Best for Custom Exploits)
When you need to dynamically compute hashes, solve cryptographic challenges, or chain attacks sequentially based on the server's response.

* Python: requests, aiohttp, urllib3, and httpx.
* JavaScript / TypeScript: Axios, Fetch API, and Got.
* Go: The native net/http package (heavily used for high-velocity custom tools).
* Ruby: rest-client and net/http.
* PHP: cURL library or file_get_contents() streams. [21, 22, 23, 24, 25] 

## 8. Target-Specific Exploitation Engines
Specialized tools that generate and send contextual payloads to compromise specific backend components.

* sqlmap: Sends complex SQL strings to extract backend database records.
* Commix: Generates operating system command injections (e.g., shell pipes, delimiters).
* Impacket: A collection of Python classes for working with low-level network protocols (SMB, MSRPC) to send exploit data to Windows servers. [26] 

------------------------------
## Master Selection Summary

| If your input data format is... | The most effective tool to send it is: |
|---|---|
| A raw web request you want to edit | Burp Suite Repeater or Caido |
| A quick text/JSON string via terminal | curl or HTTPie |
| A complex API call (JSON/GraphQL) | Postman or Insomnia |
| Thousands of strings from a wordlist | ffuf or Burp Suite Intruder |
| A completely custom TCP binary packet | Netcat or Scapy |
| A dynamic script reacting to responses | Python (requests) |

